! ISR=1 with SYNCH_RAD=1 at 2 GeV on two elements with different N_STEPS: one random number a step, from
! random_2 in elegant's order (gauss_rn_lim, cut at 3 sigma), so particle i's numbers start after all those of
! particles 0 to i-1. A QUAD with ISR after them checks that random_2 moved by exactly the numbers used. Only
! rounding may differ: a wrong offset changes the particles by the ISR spread.
Q1: QUAD, L=0.1, K1=0.5
D: DRIF, L=0.2
LM1: LSRMDLTR, L=0.5, PERIODS=10, BU=1.0, N_STEPS=800, ACCURACY=1e-6, FIELD_EXPANSION="exact", SYNCH_RAD=1, ISR=1, &
    LASER_PEAK_POWER=1e6, LASER_W0=3e-4
LM2: LSRMDLTR, L=0.5, PERIODS=10, BU=1.0, N_STEPS=650, ACCURACY=1e-6, SYNCH_RAD=1, ISR=1
QR: KQUAD, L=0.3, K1=0.5, N_KICKS=4, SYNCH_RAD=1, ISR=1
BL: LINE=(Q1, D, LM1, D, LM2, D, QR)
